TI  - Knockdown of chitinase genes enhances larvae susceptibility to Beauveria bassiana in Spodoptera frugiperda.
PG  - 106796
LID - S0048-3575(25)00509-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.pestbp.2025.106796 [doi]
AB  - Chitinases are essential for chitin degradation during the molting process, making them attractive RNAi targets for pest control. The integration of RNAi with entomopathogenic fungus represents a promising strategy for insect pest control. Functional characterization of chitinase genes in Spodoptera frugiperda was conducted to develop synergistic pest control strategies with Beauveria bassiana. In this study, a total of 11 chitinase genes from S. frugiperda were identified and characterized. Phylogenetic analysis classified the chitinase into ten groups (I-VII, IX, X and h). Expression profiling showed that most chitinase genes were expressed in the integument or midgut. RNA interference (RNAi) results showed that silencing of SfCht5, SfCht7, and SfCht10 resulted in high larval mortality, whereas injection of dsRNAs targeting the remaining SfCht genes induced neither mortality nor morphological defects in S. frugiperda. Furthermore, knockdown of SfCht genes (excluding SfCht8 and SfCht-h) followed by B. bassiana infection, ca used a significant reduction in S. frugiperda larval survival. Taking together, this study demonstrates that SfChts could be optimal targets in S. frugiperda control by combining RNAi and entomopathogenic fungus.
